<hi2txt label="Cruis'n World">
  <!--
    Cruis'n World (crusnwld) - Midway, 1996 (midvunit.cpp / V-Unit hardware).
    Like Cruis'n USA, the GOOD TIMES best-times boards persist natively to CMOS,
    saved by MAME as nvram\crusnwld\nvram (32768 bytes). One board PER COURSE
    (14 courses) plus a final overall cross-country ("OVERALL") board: 15 boards.
    Proven by matching stored values to the on-screen boards (e.g. Japan #1 ELP
    01:54:70, #10 YOU 02:00:76; overall #1 DRJ 30:33:40, #10 YOU 40:19:81).

    Boards begin at offset 0x0fe0, back-to-back, each 10 records x 0x20 (32)
    bytes => 320 bytes per board, 15 boards = 4800 bytes (ends at 0x22a0 where
    the 0xff fill begins). Each 32-byte record:
      [0..11]  leading zeros + a redundant (tick>>8) copy -> skipped
      [12..15] best time: 32-bit little-endian tick count @ ~55 Hz (overall
               needs 3 bytes; byte 15 is always 0 -> safe to read 4)
      [16..18] 3 initials, plain ASCII, one byte each (e.g. 45 4c 50 = "ELP")
      [19]     char-count byte (0x03); [20..31] cascading redundant copies -> skipped

    Displayed time = trunc(tick * 20 / 11) centiseconds, shown as MM:SS:CC
    (verified against every snapshot). Course boards carry identical factory
    defaults where two courses share one default table (Japan/Germany,
    England/Mexico, Africa/France); titles follow the snapshot subtitles. The
    15th board is the overall cross-country run (no snapshot; decodes cleanly).

    NOTE: the stylized digital initials font makes some letters ambiguous on
    screen (E looks like F, D like O, V like U); the CMOS bytes are authoritative
    (ELP/DRJ/.../"V P"/YOU).

    Authored by Pipmick @ CoinOPS.
  //-->

